WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:06.394
This is the rapid-fire track: every case answer in this episode, back to back,

2
00:00:06.394 --> 00:00:08.567
one breath each, in booklet order.

3
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:11.059
It is made for the morning of the exam.

4
00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:14.893
If one of these sounds unfamiliar, go back to its own track.

5
00:00:16.343 --> 00:00:18.049
<v Examiner>Case 2, lymph node removal.

6
00:00:18.499 --> 00:00:20.859
The lymphatic system has three jobs.

7
00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:23.415
Digestive: lacteals absorb dietary fat.

8
00:00:23.415 --> 00:00:27.545
Immunological: the thymus, the bone marrow and the lymph nodes.

9
00:00:27.545 --> 00:00:32.396
And fluid balance: draining excess tissue fluid back to the venous system.

10
00:00:32.396 --> 00:00:38.033
Cancer spreading is metastasis, and cells in the lymph can reach any part of the body.

11
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:43.932
The axillary nodes are checked, the sentinel node first: cancer there means it has spread;

12
00:00:43.932 --> 00:00:46.882
none means it's likely limited to the breast.

13
00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:51.339
Removing the nodes blocks the arm's drainage to the subclavian vein,

14
00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:54.092
so fluid stays in the tissue: lymphoedema.

15
00:00:55.342 --> 00:00:56.899
<v Examiner>Case 3, the asthma attack.

16
00:00:57.349 --> 00:01:01.509
Difficulty breathing is dyspnoea: dys, bad, pnoea, breathing.

17
00:01:01.509 --> 00:01:07.647
Three things narrow Ane's airways: bronchoconstriction, with the smooth muscle contracted;

18
00:01:07.647 --> 00:01:11.193
inflammation, a swollen airway wall; and more mucus.

19
00:01:11.193 --> 00:01:15.899
Airway resistance is high, so it needs a greater pressure difference,

20
00:01:15.899 --> 00:01:20.263
and the accessory muscles are recruited: more work of breathing.

21
00:01:20.263 --> 00:01:22.514
The reliever is a bronchodilator:

22
00:01:22.514 --> 00:01:26.401
it relaxes the airway smooth muscle so the airway widens.

23
00:01:26.401 --> 00:01:30.083
A preventer is a corticosteroid, an anti-inflammatory.

24
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:34.085
<v Examiner>Case 3, the airway defences and blood pressure.

25
00:01:34.535 --> 00:01:40.022
The defences: mucus and cilia, the muco-ciliary escalator; nasal hairs that filter;

26
00:01:40.022 --> 00:01:45.509
the carina, which triggers the cough reflex; and the epiglottis, for large objects.

27
00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:50.269
The escalator: cilia on the respiratory epithelium beat to sweep sticky,

28
00:01:50.269 --> 00:01:52.319
dust-trapping mucus up and out.

29
00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:58.070
Smoking damages the cilia, so mucus can't be moved, and a smoker's cough replaces them.

30
00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:03.557
Blood pressure: the carotid and aortic baroreceptors sense the stretch and tell the

31
00:02:03.557 --> 00:02:04.747
medulla oblongata.

32
00:02:04.747 --> 00:02:10.565
The cardio-inhibitory centre is stimulated and the cardio-acceleratory centre inhibited,

33
00:02:10.565 --> 00:02:15.192
and the vagus nerve slows the rate and force, so cardiac output drops.

34
00:02:15.192 --> 00:02:20.283
The vasomotor centre causes vasodilation, and the pressure returns to normal.

35
00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:23.623
<v Examiner>Case 3, the four lung micrographs.

36
00:02:24.073 --> 00:02:29.133
The trachea and a bronchus: ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium,

37
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:31.389
with cartilage and smooth muscle.

38
00:02:31.389 --> 00:02:36.244
A bronchiole: cuboidal epithelium, with smooth muscle but no cartilage.

39
00:02:36.244 --> 00:02:41.167
The alveoli: type one pneumocytes, squamous epithelium for gas exchange;

40
00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:45.816
type two pneumocytes for surfactant; macrophages to keep them clean;

41
00:02:45.816 --> 00:02:47.662
and no cartilage or muscle.

42
00:02:47.662 --> 00:02:52.517
Bronchodilators relax the smooth muscle; daily cortisol, the preventer,

43
00:02:52.517 --> 00:02:54.226
reduces the inflammation.

44
00:02:55.476 --> 00:02:57.161
<v Examiner>Case 4, breathing in and out.

45
00:02:57.611 --> 00:03:01.676
Inhalation: the diaphragm and external intercostals contract,

46
00:03:01.676 --> 00:03:07.407
the thoracic cavity enlarges, the intrapleural and then intrapulmonary pressures fall,

47
00:03:07.407 --> 00:03:11.339
by Boyle's law, and air flows in from high pressure to low.

48
00:03:11.339 --> 00:03:17.336
Exhalation: they relax, the elastic lungs recoil passively, intrapulmonary pressure rises,

49
00:03:17.336 --> 00:03:18.535
and air flows out.

50
00:03:18.535 --> 00:03:22.667
Forced breathing in adds the sternocleidomastoid and scalenes;

51
00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:27.598
forced breathing out uses the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals.

52
00:03:27.598 --> 00:03:30.597
Ventilation gets harder with blocked airways,

53
00:03:30.597 --> 00:03:35.994
with stiff lungs from scar tissue or no surfactant, and with a lost pleural seal:

54
00:03:35.994 --> 00:03:38.260
a pneumothorax collapses the lung.

55
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.771
<v Examiner>Case 4, breathing and blood pH.

56
00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:47.304
Normal blood pH is seven point three five to seven point four five.

57
00:03:47.304 --> 00:03:53.752
The buffer: carbon dioxide plus water, carbonic acid, hydrogen ions plus bicarbonate,

58
00:03:53.752 --> 00:03:54.510
both ways.

59
00:03:54.510 --> 00:04:01.338
Breathing sets the blood's carbon dioxide, and hydrogen ions follow it, so pH follows too.

60
00:04:01.338 --> 00:04:07.786
Manaia's hyperventilation blows off carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions fall, and pH rises:

61
00:04:07.786 --> 00:04:09.455
respiratory alkalosis.

62
00:04:09.455 --> 00:04:13.475
Hypoventilation is the reverse: respiratory acidosis.

63
00:04:13.475 --> 00:04:20.151
In metabolic acidosis the body hyperventilates, to breathe off the extra carbon dioxide;

64
00:04:20.151 --> 00:04:24.171
in metabolic alkalosis it hypoventilates, to keep it.

65
00:04:25.421 --> 00:04:27.619
<v Examiner>Case 4, the two acid-base tables.

66
00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:30.832
Metabolic acidosis: hydrogen ions up;

67
00:04:30.832 --> 00:04:36.806
the buffer converts them into carbon dioxide and water, so carbon dioxide rises,

68
00:04:36.806 --> 00:04:38.599
and breathing speeds up.

69
00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:41.586
Metabolic alkalosis: hydrogen ions down;

70
00:04:41.586 --> 00:04:47.337
the buffer makes them from carbon dioxide and water, so carbon dioxide falls,

71
00:04:47.337 --> 00:04:48.830
and breathing slows.

72
00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:54.207
Hyperventilation: carbon dioxide down; the buffer uses up hydrogen ions,

73
00:04:54.207 --> 00:04:56.971
so they fall and pH rises: alkalosis.

74
00:04:56.971 --> 00:05:03.692
Hypoventilation: carbon dioxide up; the buffer makes hydrogen ions, so pH falls: acidosis.

75
00:05:03.692 --> 00:05:06.306
Compare them: it's the same buffer,

76
00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:10.637
and carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions always move together.

77
00:05:11.887 --> 00:05:13.551
<v Examiner>Case 6, what insulin does.

78
00:05:14.001 --> 00:05:20.112
Insulin is made by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans, in the endocrine pancreas,

79
00:05:20.112 --> 00:05:24.026
and it lowers blood sugar; the alpha cells make glucagon.

80
00:05:24.026 --> 00:05:28.215
Its opponents: glucagon, adrenaline from the adrenal medulla,

81
00:05:28.215 --> 00:05:33.364
cortisol from the adrenal cortex, growth hormone, and the thyroid hormones.

82
00:05:33.364 --> 00:05:37.484
At a cell, insulin travels in the blood, binds its receptor,

83
00:05:37.484 --> 00:05:43.664
and a cascade moves GLUT 4 into the membrane, letting glucose in by facilitated diffusion.

84
00:05:43.664 --> 00:05:48.401
In the liver and muscles it drives glycogenesis, glucose to glycogen.

85
00:05:48.401 --> 00:05:50.255
In fat tissue, lipogenesis.

86
00:05:51.505 --> 00:05:53.660
<v Examiner>Case 6, glucagon and the day graph.

87
00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:58.714
Glucagon raises blood glucose three ways: glycogenolysis, lipolysis,

88
00:05:58.714 --> 00:06:00.948
and gluconeogenesis in the liver.

89
00:06:00.948 --> 00:06:04.130
Over the day, blood glucose follows your meals,

90
00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:10.155
but it never hits zero and stays in the normal range, and insulin never hits zero either.

91
00:06:10.155 --> 00:06:14.556
Basal insulin is the background; bolus insulin comes after meals.

92
00:06:14.556 --> 00:06:18.482
Normally, blood glucose never goes above the normal range.

93
00:06:18.482 --> 00:06:22.003
It's lowest overnight, so breakfast breaks the fast.

94
00:06:22.003 --> 00:06:28.028
Insulin resistance overworks the pancreas until it fatigues and makes too little insulin:

95
00:06:28.028 --> 00:06:31.345
type 2 diabetes, and possibly insulin injections.

96
00:06:32.595 --> 00:06:33.833
<v Examiner>Case 8, the joint.

97
00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:38.501
The joint, letter by letter: muscle contracts to move the bones;

98
00:06:38.501 --> 00:06:42.851
the enthesis is where ligaments attach; synovial fluid lubricates;

99
00:06:42.851 --> 00:06:47.399
a bursa reduces friction; the joint capsule holds the bones together;

100
00:06:47.399 --> 00:06:51.617
a tendon joins muscle to bone; bone is a lever that articulates;

101
00:06:51.617 --> 00:06:57.549
hyaline cartilage reduces friction and absorbs shock; and a ligament holds bones together.

102
00:06:57.549 --> 00:06:59.856
Synovial joints are freely movable.

103
00:06:59.856 --> 00:07:05.458
The shoulder and hip are ball-and-socket; the knee and elbow are hinges, like a door.

104
00:07:05.458 --> 00:07:10.665
Bursitis is an inflamed, swollen bursa, the friction cushion, and it's painful.

105
00:07:11.915 --> 00:07:14.752
<v Examiner>Case 8, rheumatoid arthritis and cortisol.

106
00:07:15.202 --> 00:07:17.495
Rheumatoid arthritis is autoimmune.

107
00:07:17.495 --> 00:07:20.443
It comes on over weeks to months, at any age,

108
00:07:20.443 --> 00:07:24.701
affects joints on both sides with morning stiffness over an hour,

109
00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:26.667
and brings whole-body fatigue.

110
00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:31.777
Osteoarthritis is wear and tear: later in life, over years, one set of joints,

111
00:07:31.777 --> 00:07:36.297
little swelling, stiffness under an hour, and no whole-body symptoms.

112
00:07:36.297 --> 00:07:40.032
Cortisol comes from the adrenal cortex, released by ACTH.

113
00:07:40.032 --> 00:07:42.849
It raises blood glucose by gluconeogenesis,

114
00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:48.286
keeps blood volume and perfusion up through long-term stress, reduces inflammation,

115
00:07:48.286 --> 00:07:52.479
and suppresses the immune system, which is how it helps Shannon.

116
00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:56.213
And tell her: it's autoimmune, and it can hit at any age.

117
00:07:57.463 --> 00:07:58.829
<v Examiner>Case 9, the synapse.

118
00:07:59.279 --> 00:08:04.830
A neurotransmitter is a chemical released at the presynaptic endings of a chemical

119
00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:09.162
synapse, which acts on the receptors of the postsynaptic neuron.

120
00:08:09.162 --> 00:08:13.021
Dopamine is made in the substantia nigra of the midbrain.

121
00:08:13.021 --> 00:08:17.760
The action potential reaches the axon terminal, calcium channels open,

122
00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:23.514
and calcium makes the vesicles release neurotransmitter into the cleft by exocytosis.

123
00:08:23.514 --> 00:08:27.778
It diffuses across, binds receptors on the postsynaptic neuron,

124
00:08:27.778 --> 00:08:32.314
and changes the membrane's permeability: excitatory, or inhibitory.

125
00:08:32.314 --> 00:08:36.173
Then it's cleared, by re-uptake or enzymatic destruction.

126
00:08:37.423 --> 00:08:40.089
<v Examiner>Case 9, the synapse labels and levodopa.

127
00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:42.572
Letter A, a calcium channel.

128
00:08:42.572 --> 00:08:44.968
B, a vesicle of neurotransmitter.

129
00:08:44.968 --> 00:08:47.436
C, the neurotransmitter molecules.

130
00:08:47.436 --> 00:08:49.033
D, a reuptake channel.

131
00:08:49.033 --> 00:08:50.268
E, the receptors.

132
00:08:50.268 --> 00:08:53.535
F, the presynaptic neuron, with the vesicles.

133
00:08:53.535 --> 00:08:56.947
G, the postsynaptic neuron, with the receptors.

134
00:08:56.947 --> 00:09:01.085
Into the cleft by exocytosis, and across it by diffusion.

135
00:09:01.085 --> 00:09:07.474
Levodopa, not dopamine, because a drug must cross the blood-brain barrier to work on the

136
00:09:07.474 --> 00:09:07.909
brain.

137
00:09:07.909 --> 00:09:13.572
Levodopa crosses and is made into dopamine there; dopamine itself can't cross.

138
00:09:14.822 --> 00:09:16.614
<v Examiner>Case 10, stroke and the brain.

139
00:09:17.064 --> 00:09:20.021
Area A, the prefrontal cortex: judgement,

140
00:09:20.021 --> 00:09:24.348
so damage brings poor judgement and inappropriate behaviour.

141
00:09:24.348 --> 00:09:26.800
B, Broca's area: producing speech.

142
00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.343
C, the motor area, on the precentral gyrus: voluntary movement.

143
00:09:31.343 --> 00:09:35.598
D, the somatosensory area, on the postcentral gyrus: touch.

144
00:09:35.598 --> 00:09:37.761
E, the auditory area: hearing.

145
00:09:37.761 --> 00:09:40.862
F, Wernicke's area: understanding language.

146
00:09:40.862 --> 00:09:43.025
G, the occipital area: vision.

147
00:09:43.025 --> 00:09:45.045
Lose the area, lose its job.

148
00:09:45.045 --> 00:09:47.785
Drunk with no alcohol: the cerebellum.

149
00:09:47.785 --> 00:09:50.309
Long, gasping in-breaths: the pons.

150
00:09:50.309 --> 00:09:54.059
Very slow, shallow breathing: the medulla oblongata.

151
00:09:54.059 --> 00:09:57.521
Hands acting independently: the corpus callosum.

152
00:09:58.771 --> 00:10:01.672
<v Examiner>Case 13, ovulation and the cycle graph.

153
00:10:02.122 --> 00:10:08.240
The ovulation predictor kit catches the peak in LH in the urine, just before ovulation.

154
00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:14.287
At ovulation, an LH peak makes the Graafian follicle burst, the ovarian wall ruptures,

155
00:10:14.287 --> 00:10:19.842
and a secondary oocyte is pushed out toward the fimbriae of the fallopian tube.

156
00:10:19.842 --> 00:10:22.303
The fertile days: ten to seventeen.

157
00:10:22.303 --> 00:10:26.593
Each hormone drives its own process: FSH grows the follicles,

158
00:10:26.593 --> 00:10:30.320
oestrogen thickens the lining, LH triggers ovulation,

159
00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.343
and progesterone prepares for implantation.

160
00:10:33.343 --> 00:10:37.703
In pregnancy, the drop at about day twenty-one doesn't happen:

161
00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:40.445
oestrogen and progesterone keep rising.

162
00:10:41.695 --> 00:10:44.895
<v Examiner>Case 13, contraception and the open questions.

163
00:10:45.345 --> 00:10:49.412
Progesterone contraception stops ovulation by blocking LH,

164
00:10:49.412 --> 00:10:54.321
thickens the cervical mucus to keep sperm out, and imitates pregnancy.

165
00:10:54.321 --> 00:11:00.632
Hormonal methods, the pill, the Jadelle implant and the mini pill, prevent pregnancy only.

166
00:11:00.632 --> 00:11:06.733
Condoms are a barrier, and the only method that also stops STIs, though not pubic lice.

167
00:11:06.733 --> 00:11:11.080
The IUD may prevent implantation, and prevents pregnancy only.

168
00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:16.410
Fertility needs healthy sperm and hormones in a male, and regular ovulation,

169
00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.495
open tubes and a healthy lining in a female.

170
00:11:19.495 --> 00:11:24.053
IVF fertilises eggs in a lab and places the embryo in the uterus,

171
00:11:24.053 --> 00:11:30.013
and the FSH injections grow several follicles at once, so more eggs can be collected.

172
00:11:31.263 --> 00:11:34.229
<v Examiner>Case 14, rods, cones and colour blindness.

173
00:11:34.679 --> 00:11:39.671
Cones: colour, central vision, in good light, with more detail: photopic.

174
00:11:39.671 --> 00:11:42.817
Rods: the grey scale, more sensitive to light,

175
00:11:42.817 --> 00:11:46.715
working in dim light and for peripheral vision: scotopic.

176
00:11:46.715 --> 00:11:49.861
The macula has both; the fovea has only cones.

177
00:11:49.861 --> 00:11:55.674
Red-green colour blindness is a genetic defect of the retina's cones: faulty L cones,

178
00:11:55.674 --> 00:12:00.598
for long wavelength red light, and M cones, for medium wavelength green,

179
00:12:00.598 --> 00:12:03.334
so red and green are hard to tell apart.

180
00:12:03.334 --> 00:12:05.727
A colour-blindness test detects it.

181
00:12:06.977 --> 00:12:08.897
<v Examiner>Case 14, Simon's family.

182
00:12:09.347 --> 00:12:12.178
Grandfather, X little b, Y: colour blind.

183
00:12:12.178 --> 00:12:18.047
Grandmother, X big B, X little b: a carrier, because she has a colour-blind daughter.

184
00:12:18.047 --> 00:12:20.049
Aunt, X little b, X little b.

185
00:12:20.049 --> 00:12:21.292
Uncle, X big B, Y.

186
00:12:21.292 --> 00:12:25.158
Mum, X big B, X little b: a carrier, with normal vision.

187
00:12:25.158 --> 00:12:26.263
Dad, X big B, Y.

188
00:12:26.263 --> 00:12:31.096
Simon, X little b, Y: his X came from his mum, and his Y from his dad.

189
00:12:31.096 --> 00:12:36.136
It's X-linked recessive: carried by mothers, shown in half of their sons,

190
00:12:36.136 --> 00:12:42.074
a woman needs both X's, fathers never pass it to sons and always pass it to daughters,

191
00:12:42.074 --> 00:12:44.076
and it can skip a generation.

192
00:12:44.076 --> 00:12:48.771
Simon will pass it to none of his sons, and to all of his daughters,

193
00:12:48.771 --> 00:12:50.359
as unaffected carriers.

194
00:12:51.609 --> 00:12:53.635
<v Examiner>Case 15, genetic diseases.

195
00:12:54.085 --> 00:12:59.700
Cystic fibrosis is autosomal recessive, on chromosome seven: it skips generations,

196
00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:04.151
there are carriers, and the affected person is little r little r.

197
00:13:04.151 --> 00:13:09.971
Huntington's is autosomal dominant, on chromosome four: it shows in every generation,

198
00:13:09.971 --> 00:13:13.943
and the affected person is big R little r, or big R big R.

199
00:13:13.943 --> 00:13:18.873
On a recessive chart, label the affected people little r little r first;

200
00:13:18.873 --> 00:13:21.475
their unaffected parents are carriers.

201
00:13:21.475 --> 00:13:25.583
A child is affected only if both parents pass on a little r:

202
00:13:25.583 --> 00:13:28.254
two carriers give twenty-five per cent.

203
00:13:28.254 --> 00:13:34.006
On a dominant chart, affected is big H little h and unaffected is little h little h.

204
00:13:34.006 --> 00:13:36.608
An unaffected cousin can't pass it on;

205
00:13:36.608 --> 00:13:40.716
an affected one has a fifty per cent chance with each child.

206
00:13:41.966 --> 00:13:43.104
That's every answer.

207
00:13:43.104 --> 00:13:44.014
Go and get them.
